<section class="cxx entity object">
<h1 id="Arcadia_ByteArray">Arcadia_ByteArray</h1>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArray</code> extends <code>Arcadia_Object</code>.</p>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArray</code> represents a sequence of bytes whose supported operations are described by stable capabilities.</p>
<p>Every instance reports whether it supports mutation (<code>Arcadia_ByteArray_isMutable</code>),
resizing (<code>Arcadia_ByteArray_isResizable</code>) and contiguous storage
(<code>Arcadia_ByteArray_isContiguous</code>). These properties do not change during
an instance's lifetime. They are independent of memory ownership.</p>
<p>The common <code>Arcadia_ByteArray_setAt</code> and
<code>Arcadia_ByteArray_resize</code> operations raise
<code>Arcadia_Status_OperationInvalid</code> when unsupported, before validating
an index or requested size. Invalid indexes of supporting implementations
must raise <code>Arcadia_Status_ArgumentValueInvalid</code>.</p>
<p>The existing <code>RuntimeByteArray</code> wrapper and legacy slice views
are immutable, fixed-size and contiguous. A slice retains its backing object;
its bounds are validated without arithmetic overflow. Slices remain read-only
in this initial capability-dispatch phase.</p>
<p>Native <code>getBytes</code> pointers are borrowed; the caller must keep the
owning object reachable. Contiguity does not imply pointers remain valid after
resizing or storage relocation. No raw memory addresses are transported through
<code>Arcadia.Value</code> or PDL.</p>
</section>

